## Description

When `terminal.integrated.accessibleViewPreserveCursorPosition` is set to `always`, scrolling the terminal accessible view caused the viewport to jump back on the next content update.

On each `onDidChangeContent` refresh, `AccessibleView._render` restored the cursor position and then called `revealLine(...)`. If the user had scrolled so the cursor line was off-screen, `revealLine` snapped the viewport back to the cursor — the reported jump.

### Changes

- `src/vs/workbench/contrib/accessibility/browser/accessibleView.ts`
  - Capture the current `scrollTop` on a same-provider refresh (`previousScrollTop`).
  - For the `initial-bottom-preserve` (`always`) mode, restore `scrollTop` after re-setting the cursor position instead of calling `revealLine`, keeping the view stable as content is appended.

```ts
if (preservedPosition && preservedPosition.lineNumber <= (lineCount ?? 0)) {
    this._editorWidget.setPosition(preservedPosition);
    if (this._currentProvider.options.position === 'initial-bottom-preserve' && previousScrollTop !== undefined) {
        this._editorWidget.setScrollTop(previousScrollTop);
    } else {
        this._editorWidget.revealLine(preservedPosition.lineNumber);
    }
}
```

Scoped to same-provider content refreshes in `always` mode; initial open (jump to bottom), the `bottom`/`initial-bottom` modes, and cross-provider reopen (reveal saved position) are unchanged.

